# Find All Anagrams in a String — LeetCode 438

**Pattern:** Fixed-Size Sliding Window + Frequency Array

## Approach
- If `s.length() < p.length()`, return an empty list.
- Decrease the frequency count for each character in `p`.
- Add the first window of `s` to the frequency array.
- If every count is non-negative, the window is an anagram of `p`; store its starting index.
- Slide the window:
  - Add the incoming character.
  - Remove the outgoing character.
  - Check the frequency array and store the new starting index if it matches.

## Java Code

```java
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> findAnagrams(String s, String p) {
        List<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        if (s.length() < p.length()) return ans;

        int[] req = new int[256];

        for (int i = 0; i < p.length(); i++) {
            req[p.charAt(i)]--;
        }

        int l = 0;
        int h = p.length() - 1;

        // Build the first window
        for (int i = l; i <= h; i++) {
            req[s.charAt(i)]++;
        }

        if (isMatches(req)) {
            ans.add(l);
        }

        h++;

        // Slide the window
        while (h < s.length()) {
            req[s.charAt(h)]++; // Incoming character
            req[s.charAt(l)]--; // Outgoing character

            l++;
            h++;

            if (isMatches(req)) {
                ans.add(l);
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }

    public boolean isMatches(int[] req) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 256; i++) {
            if (req[i] < 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

## Dry Run

**Input:** `s = "cbaebabacd"`, `p = "abc"`

Window size = `3`

| Start index | Window | Anagram? |
|---:|---|---|
| 0 | `"cba"` | Yes |
| 1 | `"bae"` | No |
| 2 | `"aeb"` | No |
| 3 | `"eba"` | No |
| 4 | `"bab"` | No |
| 5 | `"aba"` | No |
| 6 | `"bac"` | Yes |
| 7 | `"acd"` | No |

**Output:** `[0, 6]`

## Complexity
- **Time:** \(O(n)\), where \(n = s.length()\). The frequency check examines 256 entries per window, which is constant work for a fixed-size array.
- **Space:** \(O(1)\) auxiliary space for the frequency array, excluding the output list. The output list can take \(O(n)\) space in the worst case.

## Key Takeaways
- This problem uses the same pattern as **Permutation in String (567)**.
- The window size is always `p.length()`.
- Instead of returning `true` for the first match, collect **every matching start index**.
- `l` is the starting index of the current window.
- **Character assumption:** `req[256]` works for ASCII-range characters. For arbitrary Unicode characters, use a different frequency representation.
